 "productionNotes": "No source consulted for this page publishes a production total for the first-generation 4Runner, a US sales count, or a split by engine, year or trim, so no total is given. Toyota's own history of the 4Runner, in its US newsroom, gives the 1984 debut and the Hilux and Trekker lineage but no volume. The Hagerty articles, the Curbside Classic history and the trail4runner generation guide give none either. An encyclopedia mirror gives a production span of October 1983 to August 1989 for the N60 series, which covers the home-market Hilux Surf as well as the export 4Runner, so it is a date range, not a US figure. classic.com lists the generation as the 1984 through 1989 model years. A sales database that publishes US 4Runner sales by year begins its table in 2005 and was not usable here. The only hard count anywhere near this truck is for its predecessor: Curbside Classic and Hagerty both put the Winnebago-built Trekker at about 1,500 units, all sold. A US list price is partly documented: Hagerty gives $14,548 for a 1987 SR5 and The Drive gives approximately $11,000 for the 1987 truck it drove without naming the trim; no year-by-year US price table was found. Period US road tests are thin here: MotorWeek tested a 1985 4Runner on television and two articles refer to that test, but no period magazine test from Car and Driver, Motor Trend or Road and Track was fetched, so period performance figures here come from later retrospectives and are labeled as such. No marque registry publishes numbers for this truck.",
